[al:新概念英语（四）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:The Past Life of the Earth]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.48]Lesson 26 | 第26课
[00:03.68]The past life of the earth | 地球上的昔日生命
[00:09.22]What is the main condition for the preservation of the remains of any living creature? | 保存任何生物遗骸的主要条件是什么？
[00:17.59]It is animals and plants which lived in or near water whose remains are most likely to be preserved, | 正是生活在水中或水边的动植物的遗骸最有可能保存下来，
[00:24.49]for one of the necessary conditions of preservation is quick burial, | 因为保存的必要条件之一是迅速掩埋，
[00:30.40]and it is only in the seas and rivers, and sometimes lakes, | 而只有在海洋和河流里，有时在湖泊里，
[00:34.84]where mud and silt have been continuously deposited, | 泥浆与淤泥不断沉积的地方，
[00:38.72]that bodies and the like can be rapidly covered over and preserved. | 尸体之类的东西才能被迅速覆盖而保存下来。
[00:44.25]But even in the most favourable circumstances | 但即使是在最有利的环境中，
[00:47.63]only a small fraction of the creatures that die are preserved in this way | 死去的生物也只有一小部分能这样在
[00:52.96]before decay sets in or, even more likely, before scavengers eat them. | 腐烂之前，或者更可能是在食腐动物吃掉它们之前被保存下来。
[00:59.09]After all, all living creatures live by feeding on something else, | 毕竟，一切生物都是靠吃别的东西活下来的，
[01:03.71]whether it be plant or animal, dead or alive, | 不管是植物还是动物，死的还是活的，
[01:07.51]and it is only by chance that such a fate is avoided. | 能逃脱这样的命运全凭运气。
[01:11.65]The remains of plants and animals that lived on land are much more rarely preserved, | 生活在陆地上的动植物的遗骸保存下来的就更为罕见了，
[01:17.26]for there is seldom anything to cover them over. | 因为很少有东西能覆盖它们。
[01:20.71]When you think of the innumerable birds that one sees flying about, | 当你想到无数在天上飞来飞去的鸟，
[01:25.56]not to mention the equally numerous small animals like field mice and voles which you do not see, | 更不用说同样众多而你看不见的诸如田鼠和小鼠那样的小动物时，
[01:32.76]it is very rarely that one comes across a dead body, except, of course, on the roads. | 你很少会碰到一具尸体，当然，除了在路上。
[01:39.14]They decompose and are quickly destroyed by the weather or eaten by some other creature. | 它们会腐烂，很快被风化毁掉，或被别的什么动物吃掉。
[01:46.22]It is almost always due to some very special circumstances that traces of land animals survive, | 陆地动物的痕迹得以保存，几乎总是由于某种非常特殊的情况，
[01:53.83]as by falling into inaccessible caves, or into an ice crevasse, | 比如掉进人迹罕至的洞穴，或掉进冰缝，
[01:59.26]like the Siberian mammoths, | 就像西伯利亚的猛犸象那样，
[02:01.40]when the whole animal is sometimes preserved, as in a refrigerator. | 有时整个动物被保存了下来，就像放在冰箱里一样。
[02:06.58]This is what happened to the famous Beresovka mammoth which was found preserved and in good condition. | 著名的别列佐夫卡猛犸象就是这样被发现保存完好无损的。
[02:13.76]In his mouth were the remains of fir trees--the last meal that he had before he fell into the crevasse and broke his back. | 在它嘴里还留着冷杉树的残迹——那是它掉进冰缝摔断脊背之前的最后一顿饭。
[02:23.19]The mammoth has now been restored in the Palaeontological Museum in St.Petersburg. | 这头猛犸象现已在圣彼得堡的古生物博物馆复原展出。
[02:29.60]Other animals were trapped in tar pits, like the elephants, sabre-toothed cats, | 还有的动物陷进了沥青坑，比如在兰乔拉布雷亚发现的象、剑齿虎，
[02:35.46]and numerous other creatures that are found at Rancho la Brea, which is now just a suburb of Los Angeles. | 以及无数其他动物，那里现在只是洛杉矶的一个郊区。
[02:43.22]Apparently what happened was that water collected on these tar pits | 显然，当时发生的情况是，沥青坑上积了水，
[02:48.29]and the bigger animals like the elephants ventured out on to the apparently firm surface to drink, | 象这样较大的动物冒险踏上这看似坚实的表面去喝水，
[02:55.69]and were promptly bogged in the tar. | 结果立刻陷进了沥青里。
[02:58.49]And then, when they were dead, the carnivores, | 然后，当它们死了以后，食肉动物，
[03:01.94]like the sabretoothed cats and the giant wolves, | 比如剑齿虎和巨狼，
[03:05.48]came out to feed and suffered exactly the same fate. | 出来觅食，也遭到了完全同样的命运。
[03:10.68]There are also endless numbers of birds in the tar as well. | 沥青坑里还有数不清的鸟类。
